pleted\":\n        self._history = self._get_new_history(self._history, event)\n        await self._put_event(\n            RealtimeHistoryUpdated(info=self._event_info, history=self._history)\n        )\n```\n\nAnd:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def _get_new_history(\n        cls,\n        old_history: list[RealtimeItem],\n        event: RealtimeModelInputAudioTranscriptionCompletedEvent | RealtimeItem,\n    ) -> list[RealtimeItem]:\n```\n\nAnd close:\n\n```python\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Close the session.\"\"\"\n        self._closed = True\n        self._model.remove_listener(self)\n        await self._model.close()\n```\n\nAnd remove `self._background_task: asyncio.Task[None] | None = None` from `__init__`.\n\nLet me also check the actual upstream repo for the exact change to match. The actual commit in openai-agents-python is likely \"Realtime session: handle tool calls inline & classmethod history\" — commit c24f1c3... Let me just implement based on the user's description.\n\nAlso, the user mentions \"it should properly handle inserting new events into the history\" — maybe I should check whether the current implementation handles the insertion correctly. Looking at the current code:\n\n```python\n        # If the item already exists, update it\n        existing_index = next(\n            (i for i, item in enumerate(old_history) if item.item_id == event.item_id), None\n        )\n        if existing_index is not None:\n            new_history = old_history.copy()\n            new_history[existing_index] = event\n            return new_history\n        # Otherwise, insert it after the previous_item_id if that is set\n        elif event.previous_item_id:\n            ...\n```\n\nThis looks fine already. But wait — does `RealtimeItem` have a `previous_item_id` attribute? Let me check items.py to confirm.\n\nLet me read items.py.","time":{"start":1788503374628,"end":1788503402251},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"typ